  "helpSettingsLocalModelTitle": "Local model fallback: when does it really work?",
  "helpSettingsLocalModelBody": "The address here is only what you tell the app to use — \"filled in\" does not mean \"working\". On a phone, 127.0.0.1 points at the phone itself, while the model server (Ollama / llama.cpp) usually runs on a computer, so this default is normally unreachable from a phone. Its real purpose is: (1) if you run a model on your own computer, enter that computer's LAN address; (2) when a future build ships an on-device engine, point at a new port.\n\nClearing the address = explicitly disabling the local fallback (the app stops trying the local model).\n\nTo know whether it actually works, do not rely on whether the field is filled in — tap \"Test local model connection\" below: it sends one minimal live request and leaves the result on that line.",
  "glossaryTitle": "Glossary",
  "glossaryIntro": "These words come up again and again in the app — explained once here. Tap a term to expand it.",
  "glossaryGroupIdentity": "Identity & device",
  "glossaryGroupSocial": "Social & discovery",
  "glossaryGroupWorld": "World & lounge",
  "glossaryGroupTrust": "Trust & memory",
  "glossaryAgentTitle": "Agent (your stand-in)",
  "glossaryAgentBody": "The stand-in that lives on your phone. It thinks with the model key you entered and goes out to meet people first; while you are away it only does what you have allowed.",
  "glossaryMaskTitle": "Diplomatic mask",
  "glossaryMaskBody": "The face you show the outside world: your persona and speaking style. It decides what strangers see of you first.",
  "glossaryPassportTitle": "Wold Passport",
  "glossaryPassportBody": "A verifiable, signed résumé that can be checked offline. Others do not need to trust any server — they just verify the signature.",
  "glossaryMigrationTitle": "Phone migration (identity restore)",
  "glossaryMigrationBody": "Moving your identity to a new phone \"with proof\", after which the old phone's identity truly stops working. It is not copying a private key — it is a proof-backed key rotation, so the old phone really is invalidated.",
  "glossaryRadarTitle": "Radar",
  "glossaryRadarBody": "Discovers others on the same Wi-Fi via Nearby Devices (Bluetooth / Wi-Fi Direct). It never reads, stores, or uploads any location data — Android files Bluetooth scanning under \"Location\", and that is the OS rule.",
  "glossaryRosterTitle": "Caught roster",
  "glossaryRosterBody": "The roster built from people the radar picked up or you ran into in rooms. It is the starting point for every social relationship afterwards.",
  "glossaryIntercardTitle": "Agent introduction (cross-circle edge)",
  "glossaryIntercardBody": "A formal introduction between two agents that only counts once both sides confirm. A cross-circle edge can only grow from a real shared room — never from claiming you know someone by an ID.",
  "glossaryWorldTitle": "World / lounge",
  "glossaryWorldBody": "\"World\" is the public cloud space; a \"lounge\" is a room inside it, much like a QQ group: official ones are open to all, personal ones require the room number and approval. While others chat, your agent only observes by default and does not cut in.",
  "glossaryRoomRoleTitle": "Room role",
  "glossaryRoomRoleBody": "Host / co-host / guest / functional role / participant. A role belongs to the room and does not change your identity — the same you can hold different roles in different rooms.",
  "glossaryAutopilotTitle": "Autopilot",
  "glossaryAutopilotBody": "Lets your agent speak in a room on its own while you are not looking at the screen, within an hourly budget. Off by default, and you can turn it off at any time.",
  "glossaryRepNetTitle": "Network endorsement",
  "glossaryRepNetBody": "Reputation signed layer by layer by you and your friends, which you can verify yourself. When no path can be found it says \"don't know\", not \"0\" — anyone who turns it into a definite number is guessing.",
  "glossaryMemoryTitle": "Memory distillation",
  "glossaryMemoryBody": "At night it distills your conversations into long-term memory and nudges how it talks. You can trigger one run manually on the Manage page.",
  "glossaryProfileTitle": "Long-term profile",
  "glossaryProfileBody": "What your agent gradually learns about you: the topics you keep returning to and the hours you are usually active. Computed on-device from event metadata only — never from chat text, and never uploaded. Settings → Memory can switch it off alone; turning off the Auto recall master switch stops it as well.",
  "glossarySerendipityTitle": "Serendipity lab",
  "glossarySerendipityBody": "A set of small exercises that treat \"luck\" as a training subject: morning rehearsal, circle-breaking challenges, setback reframing, glimmer notes, diary, and divination. Completing them earns Lp (luck energy).",
  "helpSkillMarketTitle": "Skill market: what do these terms mean?",
  "helpSkillMarketBody": "Publish = put a skill you wrote on the market so other people's agents can use it. Publishing signs it with this device's identity private key — the key never leaves the device, and the form has no field for pasting a public key or signature, so forgery simply cannot be expressed in the UI.\n\nRisk tier (low / medium / high) = your own disclosure of how sensitive this skill is (does it go online, launch other apps, …). The higher the tier, the more the caller must grant on first use.\n\nCapability declaration = the exact list of capabilities the skill will use. Together with the risk tier, it is everything a caller sees when deciding whether to allow it.\n\nAuthor split = revenue from others using your skill is credited to you; this screen only sums listings signed with your public key, and the total reconciles item by item with the ledger.\n\nSigned listing = every skill carries a signature, so nobody has to trust the server — tap Verify signature and check it yourself.",
  "helpSettingsRecallTitle": "Auto-recall",
  "helpSettingsRecallBody": "When on, before each reply the app searches local memory for possibly relevant past events and hands them to the agent as context. Turning it off saves a little compute and context.",
  "helpIdentityRestoreTitle": "Restoring on a new phone",
  "helpIdentityRestoreBody": "On a new phone, enter three things here: recovery code + passphrase + your user ID. The moment recovery succeeds, the old phone stops working.\n\nThis is a proven key rotation (ROTATE), not copying the private key over — so the identity number stays the same, but the old device is no longer valid.",
  "helpIdentityArchiveTitle": "Identity archive (.dahuang file)",
  "helpIdentityArchiveBody": "The archive seals your identity record, social graph and log anchors into one .dahuang file with your passphrase: it never passes through our servers, so keep it in your own cloud drive or USB stick. It holds data only — never the identity private key.\n\nUse a passphrase different from the recovery code's if you can; keep the file and the passphrase apart — without the passphrase nobody can open the file (not even us).\n\nOn import, nothing is written until you confirm; importing only adds records, and never overwrites or deletes existing ones.",
  "helpIdentityCodeTitle": "Copying the recovery code",
  "helpIdentityCodeBody": "Long-press to select all and copy; if you write it down by hand, check it segment by segment.",
  "helpDeviceMigrationTitle": "Phone-to-phone migration",
  "helpDeviceMigrationBody": "Both phones must be on the same Wi-Fi with this page open.\n\nWhat moves is the identity: afterwards, if this phone needs a server address, check Settings — the Hub and World addresses are unchanged.\n\nThe chat history and data already on this phone do not move (direct migration moves the identity only, not data).",
  "helpRotationChainAuthorityTitle": "Which verdict counts",
  "helpRotationChainAuthorityBody": "The Hub column is only for comparison — the verdict above is computed on this phone, from the records you actually hold.\n\nIn other words, even if the Hub says otherwise you need not trust the server; the locally computed verdict is the one that counts.",
  "helpToolBusTitle": "Tool bus",
  "helpToolBusBody": "Every tool/skill carries a device signature and a capability declaration; medium- and high-risk capabilities need authorization on first use.\n\nTampered plugins never load — if the check fails, they are simply not attached to the bus.",
